{"content":{"result":[{"rank_order":1,"title":"","context":"### AL2\n\n#### AL2 support dropped at Kubernetes 1.33\n\nKubernetes version 1.32 is the last version for which Amazon EKS will release Amazon Linux 2 (AL2) AMIs.\n\n#### Note\n\nNote that Karpenter will automatically generate a call to the `/etc/eks/bootstrap.sh` script as part of its generated UserData. When using `amiFamily: AL2` you should not call this script yourself in `.spec.userData`. If you need to, use the Custom AMI family instead.\n\n```bash\nMIME-Version: 1.0\nContent-Type: multipart/mixed; boundary=\"//\"\n--//\nContent-Type: text/x-shellscript; charset=\"us-ascii\"\n#!/bin/bash -xe\nexec > >(tee /var/log/user-data.log|logger -t user-data -s 2>/dev/console) 2>&1\n/etc/eks/bootstrap.sh 'test-cluster' --apiserver-endpoint 'https://test-cluster' --b64-cluster-ca 'ca-bundle' \\\n--dns-cluster-ip '10.100.0.10' \\\n--use-max-pods false \\\n--kubelet-extra-args '--node-labels=karpenter.sh/capacity-type=on-demand,karpenter.sh/nodepool=test --max-pods=110'\n--//--\n```","url":"https://karpenter.sh/docs/concepts/nodeclasses/index.md"},{"rank_order":2,"title":"Guide to EKS AL2 & AL2-Accelerated AMIs transition features","context":"### Supported Kubernetes versions for AL2 AMIs\n\nKubernetes version 1.32 is the last version for which Amazon EKS will release AL2 (Amazon Linux 2) AMIs. For supported Kubernetes versions up to 1.32, EKS will continue to release AL2 AMIs (AL2_ARM_64, AL2_x86_64) and AL2-accelerated AMIs (AL2_x86_64_GPU) until November 26, 2025. After this date, EKS will stop releasing AL2-optimized and AL2-accelerated AMIs for all Kubernetes versions. Note that the EOS date for EKS AL2-optimized and AL2-accelerated AMIs is independent of the standard and extended support timelines for Kubernetes versions by EKS","url":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/eks/latest/userguide/eks-ami-deprecation-faqs.html"},{"rank_order":3,"title":"Amazon EKS Amazon Linux 2 AMI deprecation","context":"# Amazon EKS Amazon Linux 2 AMI deprecation\n\nAWS ended support for Amazon EKS optimized Amazon Linux 2 AMIs on November 26, 2025. On\nOctober 27, 2025, AWS Batch changed the default AMI for new Amazon EKS compute environments to Amazon\nLinux 2023. Amazon EKS optimized Amazon Linux 2 AMIs no longer receive software updates, security\npatches, or bug fixes from AWS.\n\n**Important:**\n\nIf you have AWS Batch Amazon EKS compute environments still using Amazon Linux 2, we strongly\nrecommend migrating to Amazon Linux 2023. It is your responsibility to maintain Amazon EKS optimized\nAmazon Linux 2 compute environments after end-of-life.\n\nFor more information about the Amazon EKS AL2 end-of-life, see Amazon EKS AMI deprecation FAQs in\nthe *Amazon EKS User Guide*.\n\nFor help migrating AWS Batch Amazon EKS compute environments from Amazon Linux 2 to Amazon Linux\n2023, see How to upgrade from EKS AL2 to EKS AL2023","url":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/batch/latest/userguide/eks-al2-ami-deprecation.html"}]}}

## Recent AWS announcements (What's New)

Newer than your training data may be. When these conflict with the documentation results above or with what you remember, prefer the most recent dated source.

{"content":{"result":[{"rank_order":1,"title":"Amazon EKS and Amazon EKS Distro now supports Kubernetes version 1.33 - AWS","context":"Amazon Elastic Kubernetes Service (EKS) and Amazon EKS Distro now support Kubernetes version 1.33. This version includes new features such as stable support for sidecar containers, topology-aware routing and traffic distribution, consideration of taints and tolerations for pod topology spread constraints, user namespaces within Linux pods, dynamic resource allocation for network interfaces, and in-place resource resizing for vertical scaling of pods","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/2025/05/amazon-eks-distro-kubernetes-version-1-33/"},{"rank_order":2,"title":"Amazon EKS announces support for Amazon Linux 2023","context":"Amazon Elastic Kubernetes Service (EKS) now supports Amazon Linux 2023 (AL2023), the next generation of Amazon Linux from AWS. AL2023 is designed to provide a secure, stable, and high-performance environment for cloud applications. EKS customers can use the standard AL2023-based EKS optimized Amazon Machine Image (AMI) with Managed Node Groups, self-managed nodes, and Karpenter","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/2024/02/amazon-eks-support-linux-2023/"},{"rank_order":3,"title":"aws-containers","skill_description":"Builds, deploys, debugs, and optimizes containerized workloads on Amazon EKS, ECS, Fargate, ECR, and Elastic Beanstalk. Covers EKS operations and add-ons; ECS task definitions, services, Exec, Express Mode, Managed Instances, and Action Logs; ECR repositories and lifecycle policies; and Elastic Beanstalk platforms plus Beanstalk Cluster Mode for running containerized applications on service-managed EKS compute. Use for container deployment, service selection, configuration, scaling, health, troubleshooting, image workflows, or deciding between Beanstalk-managed Cluster Mode and direct EKS. Should be used instead of relying on internal knowledge for these services.\n\nServices: ecs, ecr, eks, apprunner, beanstalk\nTasks: deploy, debug, optimize\nPersona: developer, devops\nWorkload: containers, compute, kubernetes","skill_name":"aws-containers"},{"rank_order":4,"title":"amazon-ec2-image-builder","skill_description":"Creates and automates custom image builds with EC2 Image Builder - Linux, Windows, and macOS AMIs, and container images to ECR. Covers the build IAM role, Amazon-managed and custom components, image recipes, infrastructure and distribution configuration (launch templates, SSM parameters, other Regions), one-off builds, recurring scheduled pipelines for golden AMI automation and OS patching, custom image workflows, and diagnosing failed builds. Applies when creating, automating, or scheduling AMI or container image builds with Image Builder, or when debugging a failed build. Not for launching instances from existing AMIs, AMI lifecycle/retirement, or general EC2 fleet management.\n\nServices: imagebuilder, ec2, ecr, iam\nTasks: create, configure, deploy, debug\nPersona: developer, devops\nWorkload: compute, containers","skill_name":"amazon-ec2-image-builder"},{"rank_order":5,"title":"managing-amazon-kinesis-data-streams","skill_description":"Operates Amazon Kinesis Data Streams (KDS). Covers streaming tables and Amazon S3 delivery - serverless, fully managed delivery from a KDS stream to Apache Iceberg tables on S3 Tables or to general-purpose S3 buckets. Includes channel setup, IAM, schemas, output key templates, CloudWatch metrics and alarms, DLQ handling, quotas, and troubleshooting. For all other KDS topics and questions, search AWS documentation and blogs instead.\nTriggers: Kinesis Data Streams, KDS, streaming tables, stream to S3, stream to Iceberg, stream to S3 Tables, KDS delivery, KDS channel, CreateChannel, data channel, data freshness, dead-letter queue, KDS lakehouse, serverless Kinesis delivery, Firehose alternative for KDS, zero-ops Kinesis to S3. DO NOT USE for Kinesis Data Firehose, Kinesis Video Streams, or Managed Service for Apache Flink — use dedicated skills or search documentation instead.\n\n\nServices: kinesis, s3, s3tables, glue\nTasks: troubleshoot, debug, optimize, deploy, monitor\nPersona: developer, devops, architect\nWorkload: streaming, data-analytics","skill_name":"managing-amazon-kinesis-data-streams"}]}}"}]}], "label": "Verify AL2 AMI support status for EKS 1.33"}

{"id": "6704befa-015d-4243-8649-8cbe7e9e3b16", "role": "assistant", "content": [{"text": "Confirmed — this is almost certainly the root cause. **Kubernetes 1.32 is the last EKS version that supports AL2-optimized AMIs.** As of 1.33, EKS no longer releases (and your control plane may reject bootstrap for) AL2 AMIs — only AL2023 is supported. AWS officially ended AL2 AMI support/updates on November 26, 2025, but the version-gating (AL2 not being offered/supported for 1.33 node groups) applies right at the 1.33 cut-over, independent of that EOL date.

That lines up exactly with your symptoms: the 1.32 node group (which can still use AL2) joins fine, while the new 1.33 node group using the same AL2 AMI/launch template fails `NodeCreationFailure` because the AMI's kubelet/bootstrap components aren't compatible with/supported on a 1.33 control plane.

To be thorough, let me pull the actual node group's failure details (autoscaling activity reason, launch template AMI, EKS node group status) to confirm this is what's actually happening in your account rather than just asserting it from docs.", "type": "text"}, {"id": "tooluse_Bji3yq4R2s0Jos79GDxTWv", "tool_name": "ask_user", "input": {"question": "Want me to pull the actual error details from your account to confirm?", "options": [{"label": "Check the node group's failure details", "description": "Look up EKS node group status, AMI type, and launch template in your account", "recommended": true, "recommended_reason": "Confirms the AL2/1.33 mismatch against your actual config and surfaces the exact failure reason"}, {"label": "Check EC2 Auto Scaling activity for the node group", "description": "Pull Auto Scaling group activity history to see the specific instance launch/bootstrap error"}]}, "type": "tool_use"}], "created_at": null}
